{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 64, :comments ["<b>And she fell from upon the camel.</b> The intent is not that she fell, but the language of inclining: she inclined herself slightly to one side, yet remained upon the camel. And so the expression מעל indicates, for had she fallen it would have said ותפול מהגמל. Therefore Onkelos translated ואתרכינת — 'she inclined upon it'; the Targum of 'incline, I pray you' (הטי נא כדך) is ארכיני. And so Scripture mentions concerning Naaman (II Kings 5:21): ״ויראה נעמן רץ אחריו ויפל מעל המרכבה לקראתו ויאמר השלום״ — whose meaning is only that he inclined himself slightly in the chariot toward the runner coming. And from this Elisha said to him (ibid.): ״כאשר הפך איש מעל מרכבתו לקראתך״. And the sage R. ibn Ezra interpreted ותפל מעל הגמל — of her own will, like ״ויפל על פניו״. <b>And she said to the servant</b> — meaning, she had already said it."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:54.692913688Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}